Welcome to notebook thirteen. Until now, every value you have worked with has been here, now. This notebook adds the time dimension. A Future of A is a value that will eventually be an A, or a failure, but is not yet. The work happens on another thread. Your code keeps moving.

The good news is that you already know the shape. Future has map, flat map, and for comprehensions, exactly like Option, Try, and Either did in notebook nine. So composing async work feels like composing any other container. The new things to learn are runtime concerns. Execution context. Where the work actually runs. Eagerness. Futures start the instant you construct them. This is not laziness like Option. Combinators for many futures. Sequence, traverse, zip, and first completed of. Await. The blocking escape hatch you should almost never use. And a note on effect systems. The libraries cats effect, which provides IO, and ZIO, which provides its own type, that solve problems Future does not.

Conceptually, a Future of A is a slot. The slot is empty when you first see it. Some computation, running on another thread, will eventually fill it with either a Success of an A value or a Failure of a throwable. Once filled, the slot never changes again.

Every Future operation needs an execution context. Essentially a thread pool that knows how to run a block of code asynchronously. The compiler asks for it via a using parameter, so you do not write it at every call, but you must have one in scope. The standard import gives you the global execution context. A sensibly configured pool that is the right default for CPU bound work. For blocking input output, you would normally use a dedicated pool. More on that at the end.

So you write import scala dot concurrent dot Future and execution context, plus import scala dot concurrent dot ExecutionContext dot Implicits dot global. Then val f colon Future of Int equals Future curly brace one plus one. F is now running, or already done, on the global execution context. The current thread continues without waiting.

Two details to absorb from that small example. First, Future of a block is eager. The block starts evaluating as soon as you write that line. Holding a Future in a val does not pause anything. The work is already on its way. Second, the import of global is what supplies the using execution context parameter that Future dot apply requires. Without it the line would not compile.

The lowest level way to react to a completed Future is to register a callback with on complete. The callback receives a Try of A. The same Success or Failure shape you met in notebook nine. So you can call Future on complete, passing a partial function with two cases. Case Success of n arrow print got n. Case Failure of e arrow print failed colon e dot get message. The print eventually fires, from whichever thread the execution context chose. On complete is useful at the edges of your program. Logging a result. Finishing an HTTP response. In the middle of a pipeline, callbacks lead to the same nesting problem you saw with Option before discovering flat map. Reach for combinators instead.

The same combinators you have seen on Option, Try, and Either are on Future. Map of f transforms the eventual value. Flat map of f chains a second Future after the first. Each combinator is itself asynchronous. It returns a new Future without blocking the caller. So if fetchUser of an id returns a Future of String, and fetchAccount of a name returns a Future of Int, then val balance equals fetchUser of one dot flat map, passing a function name to fetchAccount of name, produces a Future of Int. Balance is itself a Future. Nothing has been waited on.

Once you have two or more dependent steps, the for comprehension is the readable shape. Each left arrow is a flat map, except the final one which is a map produced by yield. Familiar from notebook nine. So val summary equals, for, name arrow fetchUser of one, balance arrow fetchAccount of name, yield the string name owes balance. Each step runs after the previous one completes. Sequential.

Now to the most important Future idiom of all. Sequential versus parallel. A for comprehension over Futures is sequential by default. Each step waits for the previous to complete because each one is created inside the body of the for. If you want parallelism, you start the futures before the for, then join their results. Imagine two futures, each one taking fifty milliseconds. If you write them inside the for, like, a arrow Future of sleep fifty then one, b arrow Future of sleep fifty then two, the total time is about one hundred milliseconds. The b future was not even constructed until a completed. If instead you write val fa equals Future of fifty millisecond sleep then one outside the for, and val fb equals Future of fifty millisecond sleep then two also outside the for, then write the for over fa and fb, the total time is about fifty milliseconds. Both blocks were already in flight before the for started.

Read those two carefully. The difference between them is the single most important Future idiom to internalise. The rule is, for does not parallelise. Eagerness does. Move the construction out of the for body to get overlap.

If any step in a for comprehension fails, the whole chain short circuits to that failure. Exactly like None short circuits an Option for. Subsequent steps do not run. The resulting Future is Failure of the throwable, and the original exception flows out unchanged. So if a future in the middle of the chain throws boom, the values defined after it are never bound, and the broken future ends up as Failure of a runtime exception named boom.

Same names you saw on Try, now applied to Future. Recover and recover with. Recover turns a failure into a success with a plain value. So Future of an erroring expression, then dot recover with a partial function case runtime exception arrow minus one, becomes a Future of Success of minus one. Recover with does the same but the recovery itself returns a Future, useful for retry style logic. So Future of an erroring expression dot recover with case runtime exception arrow Future of forty two, becomes a Future of Success of forty two. Two related combinators worth knowing. Transform lets you handle success and failure in one place by mapping a Try of A to a Try of B. Transform with does the same but maps to a Future of B, useful when one branch wants to chain another async step.

When you have a collection of futures and want one future of the collection, reach for Future dot sequence. It turns a List of Future of A into a Future of List of A, completing when every member completes. If any one fails, the combined future fails immediately with the first failure. So with a list of ids, calling Future dot sequence on the ids dot map of fetchUser produces a Future of list of users. Each Future dot apply call inside fetchUser started immediately. So these are in parallel.

When the pattern is map each element through a Future returning function, then sequence, Future dot traverse does both in one step and is more efficient because it avoids the intermediate list. So Future dot traverse of the ids list, applied to fetchUser, is equivalent to the sequence version above, but with no intermediate List of Future of String.

Two more small combinators round out the set. The zip method. F dot zip of g returns a Future of a tuple of A and B that completes when both complete. Fails if either fails. Useful when you have exactly two independent results to combine. And Future dot firstCompletedOf, taking a sequence of futures, returns whichever of the supplied futures finishes first. Useful for timeouts and fallbacks. You race the real work against a small sleep future, and whichever wins becomes the result.

Sometimes you need to force the calling thread to wait for a Future to complete. The boundary between async code and a synchronous outer world. The scala dot concurrent dot Await object does that, with a timeout. So Await dot result of Future of two plus two, with a timeout of one second, blocks the current thread until the future completes and returns the integer four. If the timeout elapses first, Await throws.

Use Await only at the outermost boundary of your program. Main. Tests. A REPL session. Inside a service that handles many concurrent requests, blocking a thread defeats the purpose of using Futures at all. Worse, blocking on a Future from inside another Future running on the same thread pool can deadlock the pool entirely. Treat Await as a smell whenever you see it in regular code. The fix is almost always to keep composing with map, flat map, and for, until you reach the boundary.

A short list of the pitfalls you will meet first. All of which trip up everyone once. First, side effects in map. A map block runs on the execution context's thread, not on yours. Mutating shared state inside a map invites the usual concurrency bugs. Races. Visibility issues. Either keep map pure or use synchronized blocks or atomic primitives deliberately. Second, forgetting eagerness. Val f equals Future of compute starts compute immediately. If you wanted laziness, Future is the wrong tool. See the note on effect systems below. Third, sequentialising by accident. The for body trap from earlier. If your latency adds up suspiciously, check whether you are starting Futures inside or outside the for. Fourth, blocking inside an execution context thread. Long running thread sleep or synchronous input output blocks an execution context thread. Either use Future dot blocking around the section, which can grow the pool, or run blocking work on a dedicated pool. Fifth, holding a Future and forgetting it. A failed Future that nobody attaches to silently swallows the exception, unless you have installed an uncaught exception handler on the execution context. Always handle failures somewhere downstream.

A note on effect systems. Future is the standard library answer to async. It works, and it is enough for a great deal of code. But two limitations bite once your async surface grows. First, eagerness. You cannot pass a Future around without it already executing. Caching, retries, and explicit construction patterns all suffer. Second, no cancellation. A Future cannot be cancelled once started. Work continues until completion even if no one is listening.

Two ecosystem libraries solve both. The cats effect library, with the IO type. And ZIO, with the ZIO type taking three parameters. Both wrap descriptions of async computations as values that you compose freely, then run once at the edge of your program. Both add cancellation, structured concurrency, resource safety, and richer error channels. You can write enormous Scala codebases without them. But if you find yourself reinventing retry logic, cancellation flags, or careful manual resource cleanup around Futures, take the time to learn one. For this notebook's purposes, Future is enough. The combinator shapes you have learned transfer to IO and ZIO almost line for line. Only the runtime semantics differ.

Let us close with a small but realistic example. A parallel fan out. Fetch three users in parallel, derive a balance for each, and combine into a summary, with a fallback for any individual failure. Define fetchUser taking an id, returning a Future of String. Define fetchBalance taking a name, returning a Future of Int, with a deliberate failure when the name ends with three. Define safeBalance taking an id. Inside, a for comprehension fetches the user, then the balance, yielding a pair of name and balance. Then dot recover with case runtime exception arrow a fallback pair of the id string and zero. So one bad call does not poison the whole result. Now traverse the list of three ids through safeBalance to get a Future of a list of pairs. Map that to a printable summary by joining each pair with arrow and concatenating with commas. At the boundary, in a test or main or REPL session, block once with Await dot result and a five second timeout to see the result. You get back the string user one arrow six hundred, user two arrow six hundred, user three arrow zero.

Trace through what just happened. Traverse started three pipelines in parallel. Each pipeline ran fetchUser then fetchBalance sequentially via the for. Recover caught the failure on user three and substituted a zero balance, so one bad call did not poison the whole result. Finally, a single map shaped the list of pairs into a printable summary. The only blocking call was at the very edge, Await dot result, which is the only place blocking belongs.

Notebook fourteen brings the threads together. Error handling and resource management. How to model expected failures, using Either, Try, and custom error enums, versus unexpected ones from thrown exceptions. The try finally pattern and the using resource pattern. And how those patterns extend into the async world you just met. After that, notebook fifteen puts everything to work in the Scala for Spark setting that motivates this whole track.
